 "circuits": [],
 "equations": [],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Construction",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Four diodes are joined as a full-wave bridge. AC goes in at the two ~ leads; the rectified output leaves at + and returns at -. The + and - leads sit together on one edge of the body and the two ~ leads on the opposite edge."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Current derating",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "Full 3.0 A average output holds up to about 100 °C lead temperature, then falls in a straight line to zero near 150 °C."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Surge",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "One 8.3 ms half-sine surge may peak at 100 A. Repeated surges lower the allowed peak: roughly 27 A at 10 cycles and 7.5 A at 100 cycles."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Reverse leakage",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "At 25 °C junction the reverse current stays near 0.01 to 0.02 µA up to about 85% of rated peak reverse voltage and climbs steeply past about 110%. At 125 °C it runs from roughly 12 µA at low voltage to near 100 µA at about 108%."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Body and leads",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The mold compound is rated UL 94V-0, RoHS-compliant and halogen-free. Leads are solder plated and solderable to MIL-STD-750 Method 2026. Soldering at 260 °C for 10 seconds with 5 lb (2.3 kg) of lead tension is guaranteed. A cathode line marks the cathode end."
  }
 ],
